  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the presence of a sure outcome, but neither lottery has one, so the reason would not lead to a preference. The individual, however, does not refer to certainty or sure outcomes at all. Instead, they base their decision on the relative size of the prize and the similarity in probabilities. The information considered by the individual (prize amount and probability levels) differs from the information relevant to the decision reason (existence of a sure outcome). Furthermore, the individual’s justification (maximizing gain under similar probabilities) does not align with the rule of the decision reason (prefer the lottery without a sure outcome). Since the individual does not consider sure outcomes and the reason would not apply discriminately in this case anyway, there is no evidence the reason was used. The mismatch in both information and justification leads to a low confidence in use of the reason.",
